course: dictionary
lesson: 14099
title: Menacing: words that warn of danger
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Menacing means seeming likely to cause harm. It is a word for a look, a sound or a shape that makes you feel you are in danger. «The wolf gave a menacing growl and showed its sharp teeth.» The growl and the teeth are the clues. A wolf cannot be sure to hurt you from a growl, but the sound and the teeth show it wants to, so menacing means threatening.
explain: Menacing can also be a verb, menace, which means threatening or putting someone or something in danger. «The storm menaces the little boats in the harbour.» The storm is not a person, but it is putting the boats in danger. Test it: swap in threatening, and if the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: Stories use menacing to build fear. «Dark, menacing clouds rolled over the hills» tells you that something bad might happen soon. When you read a menacing word, ask yourself: what is the danger, and who is in it? The words around it will tell you.
explain: In real life, safety comes first. If a dog or a stranger seems menacing, do not stay close. Get away, and tell an adult you trust straight away.
fact: Menacing means seeming likely to cause harm, or threatening.
fact: Menace is a verb: it means threatening, or putting someone or something in danger.
fact: The comparative and superlative are more menacing and most menacing.
fact: In stories, menacing clouds, a menacing growl or menacing faces in the Wild Wood build a feeling of fear.
fact: If something seems menacing in real life, get away from it and tell an adult.
exercise: «The menacing shadow moved slowly across the wall.» What does menacing mean? | Bright and shiny | Friendly and warm | *Threatening, seeming likely to cause harm | Very small and quiet
exercise: «The fox menaces the hens in the yard.» Find and copy one word that means threatens. | fox | hens | yard | *menaces
exercise: A stranger keeps following you and looks menacing. What should you do? | Smile and walk closer to see who they are | Stop and ask for their phone number | *Get away and tell an adult | Wait and see what happens
test: «She gave him a menacing look.» Which word could replace menacing here? | *Threatening | Sleepy | Cheerful | Sweet
test: What does the verb menace mean? | Sing softly | Lie down and rest | Forget something important | *Threaten or put in danger
exam: Which sentence is the best example of menacing? | The baby giggled and clapped | The bread was warm and soft | The cat slept on the sunny mat | *The dark, menacing sky made the children hurry home
